@@ -3039,11 +3039,21 @@ On single-device Qwen3.5-core text serving, `--kvmem-re-rope` uses the dense
 cache slots as RoPE positions in both Legacy and Layerwise recall. It supports
 `bf16`, `int8`, `fp8`, `rk8v4`, `rk4v4`, `rk4v4-e8`, `rk2v4-e8`, `nvfp4` and
 `k8v4` on the CUDA architectures that support inference in each format.
-The native page includes the normalized BF16 key. Pressure and Query restores
-rotate it at the new slot and encode it in the selected KV format. The
-stored V and V scales travel with the page. This also applies to the MTP cache
-and continuation. The option requires unscaled 64-dimensional Qwen RoPE with
-theta 1e7. The added BF16 key plane counts against both KV budgets in bytes.
+The default native source stores one 32-bit position per token per attention layer,
+shared by all KV heads. The CPU archive keeps the first encoded K, its scales
+and that position. Resident pages at the same slot stay in place. A moved resident
+page can rotate by its signed position change on the GPU. Cold pages and moved
+pages at their reuse limit reload their first encoding. All V bytes keep their
+native encoding. Checkpoints also restore from the canonical archive.
+
+The default maximum consecutive resident moves are 32 for BF16 and INT8 keys,
+8 for FP8 keys (including K8V4), 2 for NVFP4 and Lloyd4 keys, 1 for RK4V4-E8
+and 0 for RK2V4-E8. Set `--kvmem-re-rope-reuse-limit N` to override the limit.
+Zero reloads on every move. Coarse keys incur a new rounding even on a first
+rotation from the archive. For the original full-precision source behavior,
+`--kvmem-re-rope-source bf16` retains normalized BF16 K and re-encodes from
+it at each new slot. The two layouts have separate cache identities. Both modes
+require unscaled 64-dimensional Qwen RoPE with theta 1e7 and include the MTP cache.
 
 ### KVMem statistics extension
 
